so I actually read quite a lot of  non-fiction books this year well 12 to  be precise and here are my thoughts on  each of them as you may be interested in  reading them yourself or to give them to  use your sciency friends for Christmas  and I'll try and present them in the  order that I read them so I began the  year with the case against reality how  Evolution hit the truth from our eyes by  Donald Hoffman this book wasn't what I  expected which is what I wrote on the  inside cover of this book was just  somewhat fitting given this book is a  lot about our perceptions versus reality  and questions what reality is and isn't  and what I mean by that is Donald  discusses in great depth about how we  sense the world around us and our brain  senses and processes different stimuli  and why you should take your perception  seriously but not literally now  shockingly I haven't made deep notes  from my highlights after reading this  despite the fact it was the first book  of the year that I read but I remember  enjoying it at the time at least the  first half and I would reread it if I  didn't already have a massive lineup of  books to read so just by not fairly  documenting or remembering the details  when I finished it I gave it a 4 out of  five  so that's good  all right so next up is the music of  Life by Dennis Noble and this book  Dennis considers the system level view  of life as music and uses the metaphor  to ask the questions where is the score  and who is the composer if there is a  program of life  I picked this book up from the library  after a spontaneous trip while waiting  for the bike shop to fix my flat tire  and I'm glad I did it was very easy to  read well written thought for faking and  I would highly recommend it anyway the  theme of this book is that there is no  such program the DNA sequence isn't The  Book of Life sure it's where the  information is encoded but life works  also due to information not found in DNA  and It ultimately ends up discussing the  different levels by which we can view  life and introduce us the readers to  systems biology  and that there is no privilege level of  causality and biological systems and so  since I mentioned system is biology  later in a year I read a very short  introduction to systems biology by  Eberhart Floyd I made a separate video  discussing what I thought were the most  interesting Concepts in this book so  I'll keep it short here the small book  is a good introduction to systems  biology essentially the use of  computational tools and Theory to  analyze and simulate biological  processes and see how chaotic behaviors  can come from seemingly simple systems  now the second book I picked up in the  spontaneous Library trip was the contact  Paradox by Keith Cooper which caught my  eye because of the cover and also  because I really quite like the film  contact anyway I love this book and I  felt very cool to be seen reading it in  various places well not that I really  needed the book for that but anyway this  book is indeed about searching for life  out there or messaging life so introduce  you to seti and meti projects and it  also introduced me to Concepts such as  Dyson spares but it is also much more  than that and discusses the altruism  hypothesis intelligence and I'm kind of  annoyed at myself for not making better  notes in it before I return it to the  library so I'll probably have to revisit  again uh next year  another book that I made a video in this  year is house buys think 10 lessons and  intelligence by David omandt which  describes essentially what the book is  about how to think logically and make  rational decisions David was former  director of the government  Communications Headquarters and applies  Knowledge from his time there to  illustrates the 10 different lessons  I found this book quite dense and would  have provided a more condensed summary  plus I didn't feel like I have seen much  of a difference in terms of my decision  making since reading a book but that  said I mean I don't think it's trivial  to apply some of the lessons that he  tells you but I do think it is quite an  interesting book and you may still  really enjoy all the details maybe it  just wasn't so much for me  now as you may have guessed I've been  trying to expand my horizons this year  of my reading lists and one book that  helps me remember much of my physics a  level was fundamentals 10 keys to  Reality by Frank walchek another library  book so I can't actually physically show  you it gave me similar Vibes to the  contact Paradox probably because they  both talk a lot about space but this was  another book that I thoroughly enjoyed  here Frank talks about space time atoms  quantum physics and at the end human  perception which brought back memories  of reading Donald Hoffman's book  actually  actually take that back he answered  complimentarity and what level's  description are sufficient or optimal to  understand the system be that how to fly  a hot air balloon or understand the  human body  it was fundamentally an interesting book  now one book I enjoyed reading this year  was the future of the minds by Michio  Kaku the last of the library books I  think  um I feel like I read this book around  early summer at the same time I was  re-watching all the stranger things  ahead of the new series coming out and  why did I bring that up well this book  covers many topics about how our minds  work Minds control secret government  experiments they were conducted and  obviously has references and connections  to stranger things and the sort of  powers that they possess anyway I'm  still currently making my deep notes in  this book and we'll have to say the  references could be better but still  nonetheless very interesting and maybe  at some point I will make a video on  this this book  all right so then I read the end of  everything which sounds a little bit  depressing written by Katie Mark a  theoretical astrophysicist who studies  Dark Matter black holes and early Galaxy  formation  so as you may have guessed this book is  about the end of everything and by that  I mean everything and discusses the  different ways in which the universe can  end but to understand how the world  could end you have to understand how the  world began and it also has some nice  figures to illustrate her different  views now I gave this book a bit of a  lower Mark partly because I think I  didn't learn as much and that was  probably my fault because I just read to  space books previously uh so yeah but  still a good book  now at some point during the summer I  decided to watch the film radioactive a  film about the life of Marie Curie which  was interspersed with later life events  such as schnabble and Hiroshima and I  suddenly had this mad Panic that what if  I discovered something that later led to  something really bad and anyway long  story shorts I came across this book  biological weapons to see if I could  learn anything that may help me in my  sort of dilemma process frankly I don't  think it did  um but it did give me a very in-depth  historical perspective of what has  already happened in terms of biological  weapon testing in different countries  and the consequences of that now this  book came out in 2005 so it's a little  bit healthy date now and was actually  written by the late Gene Gilman  um deceased wife of the famous  biochemist Matthew musselton who was  actually heavily involved in enforcing  regulations on Anthrax usage in the  states  there's a very dense and detailed book  and personally I really struggled to  read a set of times but again I can  imagine some people would really like  this definitely be like cup of tea  um but yeah I would really hope that  someone makes some sort of like updates  book on biological weapons or  biosecurity that's definitely a book  that I feel like is uh Missing in the  markets  anyway after that in one afternoon I  decided to read this little book called  cryobiology now despite being a slow  reader I managed to read it in an  afternoon because well it might be aimed  at children but sometimes if you want a  beginner's introduction to something you  have to take baby steps and that's what  I did it's engaging approachable  straight to the point loads of pictures  it is quite outdated to be fair and I  have already made a video on this topic  the other week so maybe you want to  check that out otherwise I don't think I  would recommend buying it unless you  have kids who would probably enjoy it  um it was just more for my own personal  entertainment let's put it that way  right and so next I read this book  called bio future confronting the  genetic era by Burke Zimmerman and I  loved reading this book because the copy  I ended up getting was a black card back  and it kind of looked like I was reading  Tom Riddle diary it was just um but  anyway in all seriousness it was a very  interesting read the book came out in  1984 and I feel lucky to sort of have a  copy of this um which I got a second  hand off Amazon  anyway as part textbook part Popular  Science and well as Amazon describes  this causes human cell structure genetic  engineering clones the biotechnology  industry and the possible benefits and  dangers of genetic experiments cool  right and despite coming out almost 40  years ago it's discussing topics that  are still very relevant today  I can't say much more than that because  I definitely need to go back over my  notes in this one so again hold out for  that and also hold out because I  literally just finished reading a book  this week called the biological Time  Bomb which is in many ways very similar  to uh biofuture  again a very old book this time written  by Gordon rat Ray Taylor who actually  the national Sciences at Cambridge a  long time before me though um and then  he entered a career of scientific  journalism  and this book actually came out in 1968  to even older and to be fair my own  current impression is that this book was  far less heavy on the science  discoveries and more based on Rat Race  assumptions that they were inevitably  occur and these include things such as  longevity organ transplants IVF mind  control genetic editing and more and  then he considers the emotional  Financial moral and social problems that  come with it  now in both these books the biological  time bomb and by your future they  definitely had some sort of ideas and  Concepts that I don't think I 100 agreed  with but I don't think that's you know  sometimes is a good thing to hear other  people's perspectives but I haven't  actually physically gone back through  with the fan came to sort of digest what  I read in both these bookshots so I  can't really say much more but other  than the fact that it was quite  interesting to read these old books and  has become a weird Niche thing that I  enjoy doing now so uh yeah that's that's  that's that  um so it turns out I read some  interesting books to share  and it's been good fun revisiting them  to make this video and many of which I  know I still have to revisit to make  some more notes um probably while I  procrastinate writing my thesis anyway I  hope that's given you some books to add  to your reading list let me know what  you read this year because oh well I  didn't really need any more books to add  to my reading list but maybe others do  and to be fair I like to to see other  people's recommendations so with that I  hope you have learned something on this  video thank you to my patreon supporters  and thank you for listening

